### Why separate scheduler from workers?

The scheduler is lightweight and timing-focused (usually one active + standbys). Workers are heavy, variable, and need many parallel instances. A queue decouples their pace and isolates failure.

```java
package com.bank.scheduler;

public class JobScheduler {
    private final SortedSetScheduler sorted;
    private final JobQueue queue;

    public void tick() {
        // Atomic claim: fetch due jobs and remove from sorted set
        List<String> dueJobs = sorted.claimDueJobs();
        for (String jobId : dueJobs) {
            queue.enqueue(jobId); // workers consume at their own pace
        }
    }
}
```

### How does the atomic SCHEDULED→QUEUED transition work?

```lua
-- Lua: atomic fetch + remove + enqueue
local due = redis.call('ZRANGEBYSCORE', 'due_jobs', '-inf', ARGV[1], 'LIMIT', 0, 100)
for _, job in ipairs(due) do
    redis.call('ZREM', 'due_jobs', job)
    redis.call('LPUSH', 'job_queue', job)
end
return #due
-- Even if two scheduler instances run, each job is enqueued exactly once
```

### How does a visibility timeout handle worker crashes?

```java
public class WorkerLoop {
    public void run() {
        while (true) {
            String jobId = queue.claim(Duration.ofMinutes(5)); // invisible for 5 min
            try {
                process(jobId);
                queue.ack(jobId); // done → remove permanently
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Crash or failure → lease expires → job reappears for re-delivery
                log.error("Job {} failed, will be retried", jobId, e);
            }
        }
    }
}
// At-least-once: job may run more than once → workers must be idempotent
```

### How do retries work with backoff and DLQ?

```java
public void retryOrDlq(String jobId, int attempt) {
    if (attempt >= MAX_ATTEMPTS) {
        dlqQueue.enqueue(jobId); // permanently broken → investigate
        return;
    }
    long delay = (long)(Math.pow(2, attempt) * 1000 + rng.nextInt(500)); // backoff + jitter
    sorted.schedule(jobId, System.currentTimeMillis() + delay); // re-schedule with delay
}
```

### How does cancellation work?

```java
public void process(String jobId) {
    Job job = jobStore.findById(jobId);
    if (job.getStatus() == Status.CANCELLED) {
        queue.ack(jobId); // remove from queue, skip execution
        return;
    }
    // Do the actual work
}
// The queue cannot un-enqueue a message, so the cancellation signal lives in the job store
```

